{"name":"Jingshan Park","map":{"src":"assets/img/map-jingshan.webp","width":1000,"height":1202},"intro":{"id":"way/29201967","name":"Jingshan Park","category":"World Heritage Landscape","summary":"Jingshan was an imperial garden during the Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties. Its hill forms a commanding point on Beijing's historic north-south Central Axis and connects the Forbidden City with the ceremonial city beyond.","story":"The hill and its garden were shaped as part of the imperial capital. Today, the summit remains one of the clearest places to read Beijing's axial plan: the Forbidden City stretches south, while the Drum and Bell Towers mark the route to the north. Jingshan Hill is included in the Beijing Central Axis World Heritage property, inscribed in 2024.","source":"https://english.beijing.gov.cn/specials/parktours/guidevisitors/jingshanpark/","pos":[0.41968,0.46845],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-29201967.mp3","seconds":24},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-29201967.mp3","seconds":23}}},"pois":[{"id":"way/26390235","name":"Wanchun Pavilion","category":"Architecture","summary":"Three roofs above the city","story":"Look up at the three roof tiers: yellow tiles are edged in green. Wanchun occupies the central summit. From the surrounding terrace, turn south to read the Forbidden City along Beijing’s central axis. The roof and the view tell two parts of the same story: a carefully arranged garden overlooking an equally ordered capital. Use the open visitor paths and steps; the pavilion is a viewing subject, not permission to enter a closed interior.","source":"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QrUZLEGvD","pos":[0.52079,0.68374],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-26390235.mp3","seconds":35},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-26390235.mp3","seconds":31}},"art":"assets/demo/art/wanchun-v2.webp"},{"id":"way/26390117","name":"Shouhuang Hall","category":"Architecture","summary":"Architecture for imperial remembrance","story":"Start with the hall’s broad roof and formal approach. The main hall has nine bays and a double-eaved hipped roof. This complex held imperial portraits and supported Qing ancestor rites. If the exhibitions are open, their recreated settings explain how remembrance was staged through objects, halls and ceremony. Follow current visitor barriers; this guide does not promise access to an interior.","source":"https://gygl.beijing.gov.cn/whgy/whgy_wsgc/201912/t20191206_885471.html","pos":[0.48255,0.06479],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-26390117.mp3","seconds":31},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-26390117.mp3","seconds":27}},"art":"assets/demo/art/architecture-5.webp"},{"id":"way/26390236","name":"Qiwang Tower","category":"Architecture","summary":"Learning at the foot of the hill","story":"Before climbing, look at Qiwang Tower near the south entrance. It was a place where students of the Qing Jingshan Official School paid homage to Confucius and his disciples. The building adds an educational story to a garden better known for its views. Appreciate its exterior from the visitor path, and check local signs for any interior access.","source":"https://english.beijing.gov.cn/specials/parktours/guidevisitors/jingshanpark/mustsee/202301/t20230111_2896681.html","pos":[0.52012,0.86923],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-26390236.mp3","seconds":24},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-26390236.mp3","seconds":23}},"art":"assets/demo/art/architecture-6.webp"},{"id":"node/5411801388","name":"Chongzhen Memorial Site","category":"Cultural Highlights","summary":"A memorial to the fall of Ming Beijing","story":"Look for the two commemorative stone steles beside the rocky eastern slope. This site recalls the death of the Chongzhen Emperor in 1644, after Li Zicheng’s forces entered Beijing. The emperor left the Forbidden City and died by suicide on Jingshan. The nearby scholar tree is not the original tree: the present tree was transplanted here in 1996. The monuments and replacement tree show how later generations have remembered this turning point. View them from the visitor path and respect the protective barriers.","source":"https://gygl.beijing.gov.cn/mlgy/mlgy_lsmy/201911/t20191129_732675.html","pos":[0.95319,0.74172],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/node-5411801388.mp3","seconds":39},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/node-5411801388.mp3","seconds":36}},"art":"assets/demo/art/chongzhen-memorial.webp"},{"id":"way/654418858","name":"Guande Hall","category":"Historic Hall","summary":"Guande Hall is part of Jingshan’s historic imperial landscape.","story":"The park served the Yuan, Ming and Qing palaces and later became a public park in 1928. Beijing’s visitor account includes Guande Hall among the buildings associated with remembrance and ancestor worship, alongside Shouhuang Hall and Qiwang Tower.","source":"https://english.beijing.gov.cn/specials/cyclingroutes/route6/along/202306/t20230609_3128995.html","pos":[0.91459,0.25855],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-654418858.mp3","seconds":20},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-654418858.mp3","seconds":19}}},{"id":"way/189309373","name":"Fulan Pavilion","category":"Architecture","summary":"The western end of the pavilion sequence","story":"Fulan is the western counterpart of Zhoushang. Its round, two-tier roof completes the pattern across the five summits. Pause on the open path to compare its curved profile with the octagonal pavilion closer to Wanchun. The sequence is designed to be seen across the hill as well as experienced on foot.","source":"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QrUZLEGvD","pos":[0.18736,0.76158],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-189309373.mp3","seconds":24},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-189309373.mp3","seconds":21}},"art":"assets/demo/art/pavilion-type-2.webp"},{"id":"way/189309374","name":"Jifang Pavilion","category":"Architecture","summary":"The matching pavilion to the west","story":"The octagonal roof has two tiers, with green tiles and yellow edging. Jifang mirrors Guanmiao on the other side of the central summit. Its paired design rewards comparison: look at the silhouette and painted beams before continuing along the ridge. Follow the signed paths between the pavilions.","source":"https://gygl.beijing.gov.cn/whgy/whgy_wsgc/201912/t20191206_885597.html","pos":[0.34247,0.72252],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-189309374.mp3","seconds":21},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-189309374.mp3","seconds":20}},"art":"assets/demo/art/pavilion-type-1.webp"},{"id":"way/189309372","name":"Guanmiao Pavilion","category":"Architecture","summary":"An octagonal green roof","story":"Count the corners of the roof and compare its two levels. Guanmiao and Jifang form the inner pair on either side of Wanchun. Their green tiles and yellow edges offer a quieter contrast to the central pavilion. Notice the repeated form as you walk along the ridge; each pavilion remains a distinct stop.","source":"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QrUZLEGvD","pos":[0.69742,0.71299],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-189309372.mp3","seconds":23},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-189309372.mp3","seconds":22}},"art":"assets/demo/art/pavilion-type-1.webp"},{"id":"way/189309369","name":"Zhoushang Pavilion","category":"Architecture","summary":"A round roof at the eastern end","story":"Look for a rounded outline rather than the square roof of Wanchun. Zhoushang has two roof tiers, blue glazed tiles and purple edging. The outer pavilion pair introduces a third shape to the ridge. Compare the round, octagonal and square forms as a sequence; the differences are easier to notice than to remember from their names alone.","source":"https://www.visitbeijing.com.cn/article/47QrUZLEGvD","pos":[0.85384,0.73184],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-189309369.mp3","seconds":23},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-189309369.mp3","seconds":23}},"art":"assets/demo/art/pavilion-type-2.webp"},{"id":"way/26390116","name":"Shouhuang Gate","category":"Ancestral Precinct Gate","summary":"Shouhuang Gate is the formal inner entrance to Jingshan's Shouhuang Hall ancestral precinct.","story":"The five-bay gate stands on a white-marble terrace with an imperial central ramp and richly painted Qing-style timberwork. The present gate was reconstructed after a 1981 fire as part of the restored ceremonial approach to Shouhuang Hall.","source":"https://yllhj.beijing.gov.cn/ztxx/lhysh/sh/202202/t20220224_2615919.shtml","pos":[0.49365,0.21059],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-26390116.mp3","seconds":18},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-26390116.mp3","seconds":18}}},{"id":"way/654418867","name":"Zhenwu Hall","category":"Historic Religious Hall","summary":"Zhenwu Hall is the rear hall of Jingshan's Huguo Zhongyi Temple complex.","story":"The temple's front hall was dedicated to Guan Yu, while this rear hall was dedicated to the Perfected Warrior, a major Daoist deity. Together the two halls preserve the dual devotional layout of this historic precinct on Jingshan's eastern side.","source":"https://gygl.beijing.gov.cn/whgy/whgy_wsgc/201912/t20191206_885561.html","pos":[0.94316,0.32385],"audio":{"alfred":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/alfred/way-654418867.mp3","seconds":17},"joanne":{"src":"assets/demo/audio/joanne/way-654418867.mp3","seconds":16}}}],"entrances":[{"name":"South Entrance","pos":[0.52561,0.99087]},{"name":"East Entrance","pos":[0.98193,0.50081]},{"name":"West Entrance","pos":[0.02834,0.53651]}],"routes":[{"id":"v2-classic","title":"Classic Highlights","minutes":97,"stops":[{"id":"node/1382551889","name":"South Entrance","pos":[0.52561,0.99087]},{"id":"way/26390236","name":"Qiwang Tower","pos":[0.53462,0.8253]},{"id":"way/26390235","name":"Wan